{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 22, :comments ["<b>And the children of Israel came into the midst of the sea on the dry ground.</b> Here it puts 'the midst of the sea' before 'dry ground,' and in the adjacent verse it says the reverse: 'And the children of Israel walked on the dry ground in the midst of the sea.' Moreover, that entire verse is superfluous. And here it is written 'וְהַמַּיִם לָהֶם חוֹמָה' — full, with a vav; and in the adjacent verse it is written 'חֹמָה' — deficient, without a vav. And in the Yalkut (Beshallah 238) it concludes that this is an expression of wrath and fury (חֵמָה), because Israel stood in judgment, whether to be saved or to drown with the Egyptians.\n\nAnd according to this approach we may say that among Israel there were groups upon groups at the sea — some of them righteous, some of them of little faith, like Noah, of whom it is written (Genesis 7:7), 'מִפְּנֵי מֵי הַמַּבּוּל' — that he did not enter the ark until the waters of the flood forced him. Now the good ones among Israel came immediately into the sea, into its very midst, even though they had not yet seen the dry ground; nevertheless they trusted in HaShem and entered into the sea, and only afterward saw the dry ground — and for them the waters were 'חוֹמָה,' a wall to protect them. But the lesser ones, sons in whom there is no faith, walked first on the dry ground, for they did not trust in Elohim; therefore they did not enter into the sea until they first saw the dry ground — and for them the waters were 'חֵמָה,' fierce fury, for they stood in judgment whether to be saved or to drown.\n\nAnd for this reason it says above 'וַיָּבֹאוּ' ('and they came'), to indicate that immediately upon coming they entered into the sea before they saw the dry ground. And below it says 'הָלְכוּ' ('and they walked'), for even though they saw the dry ground, nevertheless they did not trust, but first walked on the dry ground and only afterward entered also into the midst of the sea." "And some say that at their coming to the sea — that is, at their entry — they were completely righteous; therefore concerning them 'חוֹמָה' is written full. However, when they walked on the far shore, they were rebellious against HaShem, as it is written (Psalms 106:7), 'וַיַּמְרוּ עַל יָם בְּיַם סוּף' — 'and they rebelled at the sea, at the Sea of Reeds.' For they said: 'Just as we are coming up from this side, so too the Egyptians will come up from the other side.' Therefore the waters became for them 'חֵמָה' and fury, as stated."], :book :exodus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 14, :notes {0 "The phrase 'בנים לא אמון בם' alludes to Deuteronomy 32:20; I rendered it directly as the idiom 'sons in whom there is no faith.'", 1 "The phrase 'על שפתו השני' literally means 'on its second lip/shore'; rendered as 'the far shore' for clarity."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:20:18.177978807Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}